## 2. Part A — Copyright license (CC BY 4.0)

The Specification text and its companion documentation are licensed to the public under the **Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International Public License** (CC BY 4.0) — canonical text in [`LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt`](LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt), deed at <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>. This overview applies CC BY 4.0 by reference and does not modify it; in case of conflict the canonical text prevails.

### 2.1 Scope of Part A

Part A applies to the specification and documentation files, including their future revisions:

- `Secure-Business-Messaging-Profile.md` — the umbrella Specification document (and its future revisions);
- `ietf/draft-sbm-mls-erd-00.md` — the Internet-Draft for the wire protocol. NOTE: upon submission to the IETF the draft is additionally governed by the IETF Trust Legal Provisions (BCP 78 / BCP 79); the `ipr: trust200902` declaration in the draft controls for IETF purposes (see [`IPR.md`](IPR.md) §6);
- `etsi/TS-SBM-QERDS-Binding-v0.1.md` — the TS-shaped QERDS binding profile. NOTE: this is not an ETSI deliverable; upon any adoption by ETSI, the ETSI IPR Policy and copyright regime would govern the resulting ETSI deliverable, while the text as published here remains under this Part A;
- `README.md`, `IPR.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`, this `LICENSE` overview, and other Markdown documentation files, unless a given file carries a different notice.

### 2.2 Attribution notice

When reusing or adapting the Specification or its companion documentation, please include a notice substantially similar to:

> © 2025–2026 Paolo De Rosa and contributors. Licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0). Original source: <https://github.com/european-business-wallet/sbm-design-study>. A copy of the license is available at <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode>. Patent rights are subject to the Royalty-Free commitment described in `IPR.md`.

---

## 3. Part C — Reference code and machine-readable artefacts (MIT)

Reference code and machine-readable artefacts — all files under `scripts/`, `tests/`, `schemas/` and `samples/`, and `edd-resolver-openapi.yaml`, plus the build/CI files (`Makefile`, `.github/workflows/`, `scripts/requirements.txt`) — are licensed under the **MIT License** ([`LICENSES/MIT.txt`](LICENSES/MIT.txt)). These are meant to be embedded directly in implementations, where a permissive software license is the appropriate instrument; licensing them with the code keeps attribution obligations out of implementers' codebases. Their technical content mirrors the Specification, which prevails in case of divergence.

The reference code is provided on an "AS IS" basis, without warranties or conditions of any kind. It is **not** a production implementation, has not been independently audited for security, and should not be deployed in production without appropriate review.

---

## 4. Patents

The Royalty-Free (RAND-Z) patent commitment of contributors, its definitions (Contribution, Essential Patent Claim, Conformant Implementation), scope and exclusions, defensive termination, and the IETF/ETSI regime notes are in [`IPR.md`](IPR.md).

## 5. Contributions

Contributions are accepted on an **inbound = outbound** basis: a Contribution is licensed outbound under CC BY 4.0 / MIT as applicable, and a Contributor's Essential Patent Claims are subject to the RAND-Z commitment. A Contribution is material intentionally submitted for inclusion **and** actually incorporated — a bare issue or comment is not a Contribution. See [`CONTRIBUTING.md`](CONTRIBUTING.md) and [`IPR.md`](IPR.md) §5.

